Jahshaka patch 0028 — a reflection probe only reaches a material that can
reflect it.

WHY (owner, 2026-09-13, REFLECTION_PROBE_AUDIT.md Q1): "[reflection probes]
should only affect reflective objects in a scene." At this pin there is NO
material-side gate anywhere in the probe chain. `HlmsPbs::
notifyPropertiesMergedPreGenerationStep` sets `use_envprobe_map` and
`use_parallax_correct_cubemaps` for EVERY datablock as soon as the PASS property
`parallax_correct_cubemaps` is set, and the per-pixel probe loop
(`forwardPlusDoCubemaps`) is inserted from the PASS property
`hlms_enable_cubemaps_auto` — so a rough grey wall runs the same eight
readOnlyFetches, two localCorrects and cube-array sample per overlapping probe
that a chrome sphere does. The only conditional in the whole chain is
`pixelData.roughness < 1.0f || vctSpecular.w == 0`, which excludes a
perfectly-rough surface and only while VCT is live.

THE GATE IS THE MATERIAL'S OWN REFLECTANCE, not a roughness threshold — roughness
blurs a reflection, it never removes one. Unreal spells the same control as the
Specular input (default 0.5 = ~4% reflectance, 0 = reflects nothing) and likewise
has no per-material "receives reflections" tick.

WHY IT CANNOT CHANGE A PIXEL. The PBS specular environment term ends as

    Rs = envColourS * pixelData.specular * (F0 * envBRDF.x + envBRDF.y)

with `pixelData.specular = material.kS`
(Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Main/800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any:307). A datablock
whose SPECULAR COLOUR is black therefore multiplies every environment term by
zero, and skipping the lookup is arithmetically identical rather than an
approximation. The gate additionally requires no authored F0 (the second half of
the pair the owner named: F0 alone leaves the Schlick rim and the envBRDF bias),
which makes it a strict subset of that provably-zero case. A physically lit
dielectric has kS = 1 and never qualifies — it keeps its ~4% sheen.

TWO EXCLUSIONS, both of the same kind — a PASS feature that writes the
material's specular terms after the material has.

  * `cubemaps_as_diffuse_gi` — there a probe also carries DIFFUSE light, which
    kS does not scale. Off in Jahshaka (`setAmbientLight(..., envFeatures = 0)`),
    checked anyway.
  * `hlms_decals_diffuse` — a diffuse decal REWRITES the terms the proof above
    rests on, downstream of the material:
        pixelData.specular = lerp( pixelData.specular.xyz, decalF0, decalMask )
        pixelData.F0       = lerp( pixelData.F0,   decalMetalness, decalMask )
    (Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/ForwardPlus_DecalsCubemaps_piece_ps.any:92-100).
    So a decal laid on a matte black-kS floor IS reflective where the decal
    covers it, and gating the probe loop out of that datablock would leave the
    decal reflecting nothing in a room full of probes. Jahshaka feeds diffuse
    decals (`OgreDecals.cpp` setDecal requires a diffuse image), so this is not
    a theoretical exclusion. The gate is per DATABLOCK and a decal is per PIXEL:
    there is no finer test available at hash time, so a scene with diffuse
    decals bound keeps the probe loop on every material. (Found by the clean-2
    lane, 2026-09-13; the round-3 patch was exact only in the absence of decals.)

CLEAR COAT IS NOT EXCLUDED (round-3 correction, 2026-09-13). Round 2 excluded it
on the stated grounds that "clearCoatEnvColourS is not multiplied by kS". At
this pin it is:

    Rs += pixelData.clearCoatEnvColourS * pixelData.specular.xyz *
          ( 0.04 * clearCoatEnvBRDF.x + clearCoatEnvBRDF.y ) * pixelData.clearCoat

(Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Main/200.BRDFs_piece_ps.any:334). So a zero-kS
clear-coated material provably reflects nothing either, and the exclusion bought
the full per-pixel probe loop for a term that is multiplied away. The coat's
other contributions are outside what the gate removes: its DIRECT lobe is in the
light loop (200.BRDFs_piece_ps.any:225-239) and the attenuation it applies to
Rd/Rs is in BRDF_EnvMap, which `needs_env_brdf` keeps; `clearCoatEnvColourS`
itself is declared and zeroed under needs_env_brdf too
(800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any:80-82 and 962), so folding the coat in cannot
leave an undeclared symbol behind. Measured A/B across the fold in gi.probe_gate
(g): the clear-coated zero-reflectance panel's pixels are identical either way.

AND THE F0 HALF OF THE PREDICATE READS WHAT THE SHADER READS (round-3 defect,
found by the same suite). `setFresnel` writes mFresnelG/mFresnelB only in the
SEPARATE form; `getFresnel()` returns all three regardless, and the default
datablock's stale G/B are 0.818. Round 2 tested all three unconditionally, so in
the NON-separate Fresnel workflow — the one the owner's "not reflective" pair is
authored in — the predicate answered "reflective" for a material whose F0 and kS
are both zero, and the gate never fired there at all. No pixel differed (kS
multiplies the term away either way), which is exactly why a picture-only suite
could not see it. The predicate now reads mFresnelR alone unless
hasSeparateFresnel(), which is the shader's own rule
(500.Structs_piece_vs_piece_ps.any:283-287).

`needs_env_brdf` is untouched: it is also raised by `hlms_enable_cubemaps_auto`,
so the irradiance field's and VCT's diffuse still reach the surface.

THE COST OF A CROSSING, AND THE ONE WORKFLOW THAT PAYS IT REPEATEDLY. A crossing
flush walks every renderable wearing the datablock and recomputes its Hlms hash,
so it is not free; an edit that leaves the material reflective either way costs
nothing at all, by construction (both sides false => no flush). The one
authoring gesture that crosses more than once is a user DRAGGING the Specular
Color slider down through black and back up, which crosses exactly twice — once
at the frame the value reaches zero and once at the frame it leaves it — not
once per frame of the drag. Both halves are measured rather than asserted: the
engine counts crossings (`world.giStatus().probeGateCrossings`) and gi.probe_gate
gates 100 non-crossing pushes at ZERO crossings, and a 41-push drag through
black at exactly two.

THE PREDICATE IS A DATABLOCK METHOD, AND EDITING IT REBUILDS THE SHADER
(round-2 fix, 2026-09-13). The gate is decided in `calculateHashForPreCreate`,
which runs only on `flushRenderables()` — and at this pin `setSpecular`,
`setMetalness` and `setFresnel` call `scheduleConstBufferUpdate()` and nothing
else. A material authored black-kS therefore stayed gated after the user raised
its Specular Colour to make it a mirror: the constant changed, the shader did
not, and the floor went on reflecting nothing until some unrelated edit happened
to flush it. (Jahshaka's own material push writes kS on the live datablock every
frame a material changes and deliberately never flushes — OgreMaterials.cpp
applyPbr — so nothing else was going to.)

The fix is upstream's own idiom for exactly this situation, `setClearCoat`
(OgreHlmsPbsDatablock.cpp:885-897): evaluate the predicate before and after the
write and flush ONLY when it CROSSES. An ordinary edit that leaves the material
reflective either way still costs nothing. So that the rule cannot drift, the
predicate has ONE definition — `HlmsPbsDatablock::hasZeroSpecularResponse()` —
shared by the three setters and by `calculateHashForPreCreate`.

WHAT IT TOUCHES
  * SOURCE  Components/Hlms/Pbs/include/OgreHlmsPbs.h  — one property id.
  * SOURCE  Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/OgreHlmsPbs.cpp    — the gate in
    calculateHashForPreCreate and in
    notifyPropertiesMergedPreGenerationStep.
  * SOURCE  Components/Hlms/Pbs/include/OgreHlmsPbsDatablock.h,
            Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/OgreHlmsPbsDatablock.cpp — the one
    definition of the predicate, and the three setters that can cross it.
  * MEDIA   Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Main/800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any —
    `@insertpiece( forwardPlusDoCubemaps )` becomes conditional on the
    DATABLOCK property `use_parallax_correct_cubemaps`. Without this hunk the
    C++ half saves nothing: the piece is defined from a pass property.

SOURCE patch (not media-only): every tree must re-run build-ogre.sh.
The media file is CRLF; edited in binary mode.

